      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given conditions:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{l}\nn \\mod 6=1 \\\\\nn+2010 \\mod 6=\\_ \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nTo find the remainder when $n+2010$ is divided by 6, we consider the possible remainders when 2010 is divided by 6. The division theorem states that for any integers $a$ and $b$ with $b > 0$, there exist unique integers $q$ and $r$ such that:\n\n\\[\na = bq + r, \\quad 0 \\leq r < b\n\\]\n\nThus, the remainder when 2010 is divided by 6 is:\n\n\\[\n2010 \\mod 6 = \\_ \\quad \\text{(unknown)}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's determine the remainder when $n+2010$ is divided by 6. According to the problem statement, we are given that $n \\mod 6=1$. This means:\n\n\\[\nn = 6k + 1, \\quad k \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n\\]\n\nNow, we replace $n$ with $6k + 1$ in the expression $n + 2010$:\n\n\\[\nn + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010 = 6k + 2011\n\\]\n\nSince $6k$ is a multiple of 6, we only need to find the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6. The remainder can be found as follows:\n\n\\[\n2011 \\mod 6 = \\_ \\quad \\text{(unknown)}\n\\]\n\nThus, the remainder when $n+2010$ is divided by 6 is the same as the remainder when 2011 is divided by 6, which we will denote as $\\_$. \n\nHence, the remainder when $n+2010$ is divided by 6 is $\\boxed{5}$.\n\nThis process ensures we have correctly calculated the remainder by following the principles of the division theorem and modular arithmetic.",
